@@s027 21 December 2023, worked out
@@s028 Five problems on bitstrings. Classification: <b>Bit Shelter</b> → NP-hard, <b>Cluster</b> → graphs, <b>Decrease</b> → DP (and the counterexample), <b>Expensive Ones</b> → greedy, <b>To Xor Or Not To Xor</b> → flow. Everything that follows I have checked with programs on the examples of the text and against brute force.
@@s029 1 · Counterexample: Gordon Gecko's greedy on Decrease
@@s030 Gordon's idea: start from the bitstring with the most 1s; then, among those where you can decrement, take the one with the most 1s; repeat.
@@s031 An instance on which it certainly fails
@@s032 N = 4 bitstrings of length K = 4.
@@s033 No parity.
@@s034 The only one with 3 ones.
@@s035 Gordon stops: <b>1111, 1110</b>, length 2.
@@s036 Checked by brute force: 3 is the maximum.
@@s037 2 · Greedy: Expensive Ones
@@s038 Sort by increasing number of 1s and take while the 1s used stay ≤ R. Time <span class="m">O(NK + N log N)</span>. On the 4 examples of the text it gives 3, 2, 1, 0 bitstrings, like brute force. (The first example prints 00000, 10000, 01010: another valid solution of 3, "any will do".) Model sentence in <a href="formato.html">the 6 questions</a>.
@@s039 3 · Graph traversal: Cluster
@@s040 The grid of 1s is a graph; the answer is the largest connected component, with BFS from each unvisited cell. <span class="m">O(NK)</span>. Java code checked on the 4 examples (8, 2, 16, 0) in <a href="../teoria/basi-grafi.html">graphs, BFS and DFS</a>.
@@s041 4 · Dynamic programming: Decrease
@@s042 The answer
@@s043 If x decrements into y ≠ x, x has more 1s: in the sequence x comes first. It is a DAG, so the longest path is found with DP.
@@s044 The subproblem.
@@s045 The last step of the sequence arrives at xᵢ from some xⱼ.
@@s046 On the examples of the text: 4, 1, 3, like brute force.
@@s047 5 · Flow: To Xor Or Not To Xor
@@s048 Pairs → obtainable strings → t, all with capacity 1; d = maximum flow. Drawing on example 1, model answer and checks in <a href="../teoria/flow-gadget.html">reducing to a flow</a>.
@@s049 6 · NP-hard: Bit Shelter
@@s050 (a) Bit Shelter; (b) Set Cover; (c) Set Cover ≤<sub>P</sub> Bit Shelter; (d) each set becomes the bitstring of its positions. Complete answer, with the two cautions about the guarantees of the input, in <a href="../teoria/riduzioni.html">writing an NP-hardness proof</a>.
@@s051 How to use the other exams
@@s052 For each exam do <b>first only the classification</b> (5 minutes): which problem for which question. It is the most profitable training.
@@s053 Then write the answers with a limit of half a page each, as the text asks.
@@s054 Check against the solutions presented in lecture and the TA solutions for the NP questions (2019, 2021, 2023).
@@s055 If you want
@@s056 Bring one of these exams to the chat when you do it: I will correct the classification and the answers with you, and add it to this page worked out like 2023.
@@s057 Question 5 worked: To Xor Or Not To Xor
@@s058 Given <i>n</i> pairs of bitstrings of length <i>k</i>, I build a flow network:
@@s059 a source <code>s</code>, a sink <code>t</code>, one node for each pair <i>i</i> (n nodes), and one node for each distinct bitstring obtainable as the AND, OR or XOR of a pair (at most 3n nodes);
@@s060 arc <code>s → i</code> of capacity 1 for each pair; arc from <i>i</i> to each of the at most 3 strings it can produce, capacity 1; arc from each string-node to <code>t</code>, capacity 1. In all at most 7n arcs.
@@s061 An integer flow of value <code>d</code> chooses <code>d</code> pairs and assigns them <code>d</code> different strings. So the maximum flow is the maximum number <code>d</code> of different strings. The remaining pairs can use any operation.
@@s062 Cost: computing the strings O(nk). The flow with Ford-Fulkerson costs O(m·C) = O(n²), because m = O(n) and the value is at most C = n.
@@s063 Check
@@s064 The Java code of this reduction gives d = 3 and d = 2 on the two examples of the text. On 500 small random instances the maximum flow coincides with brute force, which tries all the 3ⁿ choices.
@@s065 Question 6 worked: Bit Shelter is NP-hard
@@s066 The other five problems are: Cluster (BFS), Decrease (DP on the DAG), Expensive Ones (greedy) and To Xor (flow, above). The NP-hard question remains, in three parts:
@@s067 question
@@s068 answer
@@s069 (a) which problem is NP-hard? (P1)
@@s070 Bit Shelter: choosing the minimum number of bitstrings whose OR gives all 1s
@@s071 (b) which known NP-hard problem? (P2)
@@s072 Set Cover: each bitstring is the set of positions where it has a 1
@@s073 (c) P1 ≤p P2 or P2 ≤p P1?
@@s074 P2 ≤p P1: Set Cover ≤p Bit Shelter
@@s075 Small instance: U = {1, 2, 3, 4}, S1 = {1, 2}, S2 = {2, 3}, S3 = {3, 4}, S4 = {4}. With K = |U| = 4 and N = 4 sets, bitstring i has a 1 in position j if and only if j ∈ Sᵢ: 1100, 0110, 0011, 0001. The OR of 1100 and 0011 is 1111, because S1 ∪ S3 = U. The minimum is 2, checked by brute force.
@@s076 The reduction: given U = {1, …, K} and F = {S₁, …, S_N}, I build N bitstrings of length K, the i-th with a 1 in position j if and only if j ∈ Sᵢ. Cost O(NK). A set of bitstrings has OR equal to 1…1 exactly when the corresponding sets cover U: so the minimum of Bit Shelter is the minimum of Set Cover, and vice versa.
@@s077 Two details on the guarantees of the input
@@s078 Bit Shelter guarantees that the OR of all the strings is 1…1 and that the strings are distinct. So first I check in polynomial time that the union of F is U (if not, the answer to Set Cover is "no"), and I remove the duplicate sets, which do not change the optimum.
@@s079 This is the kind of precision the teacher asks for: the reduction must work for every instance of the known problem, even for those that violate the guarantees of the input of the new problem.
